paragraph('31_ch07','A five-person site ranks',r'''At a site serving only a few families, one family's departure can change the completion rate enough to alter the site's rank. A funder who uses that ranking needs to know how uncertain the rate is. Similar questions arise when we combine survey items into a score, generalize from respondents to a population, or plan a study around an effect that may be hard to detect.''','Remove punchy opening and invented definitive ranking.')
paragraph('31_ch07','In this chapter we take up',r'''In this chapter we examine survey scales and weights, stabilize small-site rates, and calculate what a planned study can detect. We then build prediction intervals and estimate how much outcome variation remains between sites after accounting for measured predictors. The examples in \texttt{ch07\_trust.do} let you connect each diagnostic to a reporting decision: which comparisons the evidence supports, what uncertainty to show, and what to investigate before publishing.''','Replace exhaustive enumeration with useful preview.')
change('31_ch07','The data still speaks, but it stops shouting where it has nothing to say.','You retain each site\'s observed rate and report the adjusted estimate alongside it, so readers can see how much pooling changed the result.','Replace marketing metaphor with action.')
paragraph('31_ch07','Translated for the program officer',r'''Before adopting the shorter scale, read item~6 with the program team. Check whether its wording is reversed, whether its coding matches the other items, and whether it measures a part of engagement that the remaining questions omit. A higher alpha supports greater internal consistency in this sample; it does not establish that the scale measures engagement accurately or works equivalently across cohorts. If you revise the score, document the retained items and the rule for handling unanswered questions, then examine the revised scale in the next pilot before comparing waves. That gives the program officer a reasoned scoring decision rather than a cutoff treated as permission to use the score.''','Weakspot: alpha does not establish construct validity or group comparability; concrete troubleshooting.')
change('31_ch07','so it adds noise rather than information,','so it has a weaker relationship with the latent variable than the other items,','Avoid false no-information claim.')
change('31_ch07','Pruning the one bad item now buys comparability: this year\'s engagement score and next year\'s will measure the same thing across the waves Chapter~\\ref{ch:surveys} taught you to collect.','A shorter score may be more internally consistent here, but comparability across waves still requires checking item wording, coding, and how the items behave in each wave.','Correct unsupported measurement invariance.')
change('31_ch07','that stability is evidence that nonresponse is not driving the headline.','that stability shows limited sensitivity to these particular weights, although bias related to unmeasured differences may remain.','Weight sensitivity not nonresponse validation.')
change('31_ch07','When raking has left a few respondents carrying weights above 30 or 40, investigate or trim before publishing: those few now stand in for whole demographic cells.','Inspect the distribution of relative weights and the effective sample size before publishing. Absolute weight values depend on their normalization; a value of 40 is not by itself a reason to trim. If a few respondents dominate the weighted estimate, investigate their cells and compare results under a documented trimming rule.','Weakspot: weight scale versus concentration, concrete next action.')
change('31_ch07','\\section{Prediction intervals that keep their coverage when the model is wrong}','\\section{Calibrating prediction intervals for new cases}','Remove absolute claim in heading.')
change('31_ch07','a bad model costs interval width, never the coverage rate.','the guarantee depends on exchangeability of calibration and new cases and on the prescribed calibration quantile. A weak predictor can produce wide intervals.','State condition beside guarantee.')
change('31_ch07','their empirical $(1-\\alpha)$ quantile;','their finite-sample-adjusted quantile, taken at rank $\\lceil(n_{\\mathcal C}+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$ among the ordered calibration residuals, where $n_{\\mathcal C}$ is the calibration sample size;','Correct finite sample quantile definition.')
change('31_ch07','Across new respondents from the same population, 90\\% of such bands contain the true wage, confirmed by the package\'s held-out coverage of 0.90.','Under exchangeability, the procedure targets at least 90\\% marginal coverage for new cases. The reported calibration coverage of 0.90 checks the fitted band on the residuals used to set its width; it is not an independent test of future coverage.','Weakspot: calibration versus test coverage.')
